  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R3, R4, and R9 already apply simulated tensile deformation across temperatures to the closely related CoNiCrFeMn FCC alloy and identify partial-dislocation-mediated plasticity. R6, R17, and R18 strongly establish the connection among low stacking-fault energy, stacking faults, partial dislocations, and local FCC/HCP-like stacking in essentially the same alloy family. R1, R2, and R19 supply precedent for high-rate atomistic loading and temperature variation, while R10 and R13 connect temperature with dislocation behavior and thermal activation. The exact FeNiCrMn composition, crystallographic-direction comparison, vacancy-cluster nucleation sites, and the specific finding of rate-insensitive yield strength are not directly disclosed, but adding orientation and strain-rate sweeps to the already established tensile-MD framework would be a routine, plausible proposal rather than requiring a substantial new conceptual idea."
